WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.769
Ten Claude Code terminals. One
of them is stuck. Which one?

00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:10.760
So talk to one agent: the director.
Ask for a settings page with dark mode.

00:00:11.197 --> 00:00:17.244
A hook triages the request first: a team job,
with these angles, this model, this persona.

00:00:18.074 --> 00:00:20.698
The director hires the
team with one command.

00:00:21.073 --> 00:00:25.204
A moderator, council members with
different personas, and an integrator.

00:00:25.587 --> 00:00:28.881
Each has a name and a boss,
and appears on the map live.

00:00:29.711 --> 00:00:31.449
Now the director runs the job.

00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:35.220
Every agent keeps a task list
that ticks as work moves.

00:00:35.598 --> 00:00:38.841
The director asks a builder a
question, and the builder answers.

00:00:39.215 --> 00:00:41.756
Reports arrive: done, or blocked.

00:00:42.185 --> 00:00:46.461
The council debates, agent to agent,
and long jobs show a live log.

00:00:47.291 --> 00:00:50.797
When the job is done, the director
grades it and despawns the team.

00:00:51.198 --> 00:00:55.165
Each agent leaves a decision note.
The next agent finds it by search.

00:00:55.536 --> 00:00:57.195
Usage shows what it cost.

00:00:58.025 --> 00:00:59.915
That's what agentnet changes.

00:01:00.345 --> 00:01:06.555
Your agents stop being terminal sessions and become
colleagues, each with a name, a boss and a job.

00:01:07.225 --> 00:01:14.175
The right model for each task means less usage, better
work, and you see all of it, in an app anyone can read.

00:01:14.725 --> 00:01:17.055
You lead a team, and you get more done.

00:01:17.885 --> 00:01:20.891
agentnet runs on your own
machine, on localhost.
